  "id": "Compound::DB00786 - Gene::64386",
  "verbalization": "Marimastat, a small molecule inhibitor, is shown to bind to multiple MMPs (Matrix Metalloproteinases) through distinct interactions with Mmp23b, Mmp26, and Mmp27. These MMPs are all part of the same functional group, participating in the degradation of extracellular matrix components. The shared involvement of these MMPs in the same biological pathway, coupled with the consistent binding of Marimastat to them, suggests a potential mechanism by which the drug may influence the activity of Mmp25. The presence of Mmp25 in the pathway indicates that it is a downstream target or a component of the MMP-mediated process. The high scores in the paths suggest a strong, biologically relevant relationship, supported by the shared functional role of the MMPs and the drug's ability to interact with them. The mechanism appears to involve the inhibition of MMP activity, which could lead to a reduction in matrix degradation and potentially modulate inflammatory or pathological processes. The evidence is consistent and supports a mechanistic link between Marimastat and Mmp25, though further experimental validation would be necessary to confirm the direct interaction and functional impact.",
